          "section_id": "c_sections.0",
          "heading": "why_in_hospital",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.0",
            "sections.1"
          ],
          "text": "Your child came to hospital because she was very thirsty, passing a lot of urine, and had lost weight. She then became sick and drowsy. The team found she had a condition called diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). This was the first sign that she has type 1 diabetes."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.1",
          "heading": "what_we_did",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.2",
            "sections.3"
          ],
          "text": "When your child arrived, her blood sugar was very high and her blood was more acidic than it should be. The team started treatment straight away. This included:\n\n- Fluids through a drip to rehydrate her\n- Insulin through a drip to bring her blood sugar down\n- Close monitoring every hour\n- Replacement of important salts in her blood\n\nThe specialist intensive care team was kept informed. As she got better, she moved on to insulin injections under the skin. By day 2, her blood sugar and acid levels were improving.\n\nThe diabetes nurse and dietician started working with your family. Education has begun on carbohydrate counting, managing low blood sugar, and what to do when your child is unwell."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.2",
          "heading": "medicines",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{MEDICATIONS}}"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.3",
          "heading": "next_steps",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.7",
            "sections.8"
          ],
          "text": "Your child will be seen by the diabetes team in 1 week. A dietician follow-up has also been arranged. Phone support from the diabetes team is available in the meantime. Your GP has been told about your child's admission and does not need to take any urgent action right now."
        },
